DEVELOPER UPLOAD AND NAMING MODEL
Design note — v0.1 — 2026-10-01
Status: DESIGNED, NOT BUILT

This AMENDS docs/dbspec01, which states "Uploading changed content creates the
next version". That is upload-driven versioning and is no longer the model.


================================================================================
1. THE SHIFT
================================================================================

BEFORE: an upload IS a version-creating event. Every drop finds-or-creates the
asset, computes version_number = MAX + 1, and writes a new version row.

AFTER: the developer owns versioning in their sandbox. An upload record is a
HISTORY TRAIL entry, not a storage event. Version rules change only when an
asset moves into GAME SPACE, where the system tracks versions instead — out of
scope for now.

The consequence that matters: an upload record has value independent of new
content. Recording an upload that adds no new bytes is correct and expected —
it records "on this date, this developer submitted this, under this name".

The SHA store only gains entries for genuinely new content. Files whose SHA is
already known are REFERENCED, not stored. workspace_version_files is already
unique on (version, relative_path, filename) with no constraint on
stored_file_id, so many paths may reference one stored file. The schema already
supports this correctly.


================================================================================
2. WORKED EXAMPLE
================================================================================

  Upload                            New content        Store     Record
  --------------------------------  -----------------  --------  ---------------
  1. bigAsset04, first time         everything         all SHAs  v01 bigAsset04

  2. bigAsset04 again, now with     scripts/           one SHA   v02 bigAsset04
     scripts/nuke_v11.nk added      nuke_v11.nk

  3. BiggerAsset05, byte-identical to bigAsset04:
       nothing new
       -> WARN the developer, then record v01 BiggerAsset05 with NO SHAs written.

  Case 3 is the point. Same bytes, different role: a Nuke script may reference
  one of them by path, so they are legitimately different assets.


================================================================================
3. NAMING AND PARSING
================================================================================

The developer's folder name carries the asset name and, usually, a version
marker. The name is NOT retyped in the UI.

Separators vary — observed as . - _ or a bare v.

Candidate parser:

    /^(?<name>.+?)[\s._-]*v(?<version>\d+(?:[._-]\d+)*)$/i

    Asset_v08, Asset.v08, Asset-v08, Assetv08, Asset v08 -> Asset / 08
    Assets_v04                                           -> Assets / 04
    tree_v1.02                                           -> tree / 1.02
    bigAsset04                                           -> no match
    Video2, V2, Vault, OakTree, texture.png              -> no match

No false positives on ordinary names is the important property.

Case is PRESERVED in the capture, deliberately: it means `asset_v04` and
`ASSET_v04` parse as different names, so neither is silently merged nor
silently split — the near-match prompt fires instead.

A name with no version marker (bigAsset04) is a new asset at version 1, still
subject to the prompt below.


================================================================================
4. THREE-TIER MATCH
================================================================================

  exact      parsed name equals an existing asset exactly
             -> that asset; new upload record

  near       differences only in case / separator / whitespace / leading zeros
             (Asset_v8 vs Asset_v08, asset_v04 vs Asset_v04)
             -> ASK: "did you mean Asset?"

  different  genuinely different name
             -> new asset; its own numbering starts at 1

The prompt exists because parsing is fuzzy. A forgotten capital or underscore
is exactly the case where a developer does NOT mean to create a new asset.

Upload numbering is PER ASSET and resets for a new asset, which
UNIQUE (workspace_asset_id, version_number) already supports. No schema change.


================================================================================
5. NAME VALIDATION — THREE TIERS, SAME SHAPE AS ABOVE
================================================================================

  block   the suffix is a KNOWN file extension (.nk .jpg .pdf .png .blend
          .uasset .ma .mb ...). Such a name is a FILE, never an asset or a
          directory: nuke_v10.nk is a file.

  warn    a dotted suffix that is NOT a known type, e.g. tree.v2. Marginally
          acceptable — flag it and offer to change the name.

  accept  anything else

Reject against a KNOWN-EXTENSION LIST, never against "contains a dot", which
would wrongly reject legitimate names.

The pattern throughout is: the system GUIDES rather than BLOCKS. Warn and offer
options; reserve blocking for names that are unambiguously wrong.


================================================================================
6. CLASH MECHANISMS — TWO, DIFFERENT SCOPES
================================================================================

  WHOLE UPLOAD
    The upload duplicates an existing asset's content.
    -> warn, offer to proceed. The record is created either way.

  PER PATH
    The same version is re-uploaded (Asset_v04 twice), so incoming files land
    where files already sit.
    -> offer, per file: overwrite | rename | merge | add

  These are separate mechanisms and should not be conflated in the UI.

  See open items: "merge" and "add" are not yet defined precisely.


================================================================================
7. CONTENT IS TRUTH, NAMES ARE HINTS
================================================================================

  identity  SHA-256. Same bytes = same content, wherever it appears and
            whatever it is called.

  type      to be determined from CONTENT (magic bytes), not the extension.
            This becomes a technical check: "named .png but the content is a
            Blender file". Same principle as the SHA — the bytes are the fact,
            the name is a hint.

Both belong to the upload-types work in docs/assettypes01.


================================================================================
8. WHAT THIS CHANGES IN CODE
================================================================================

  * The upload route currently CREATES a version on every upload
    (versionNumber = MAX + 1). It must stop. NOTE: nothing else currently
    creates a workspace_version, so a replacement path is needed — this is a
    gap to fill, not just a deletion.

  * Name parsing, the three-tier match, and the three validation tiers are new.

  * Per-path clash handling (overwrite / rename / merge / add) is new.

  * filename_overrides should be REMOVED. It exists to rename files, and this
    model's rule is that paths are never rewritten. It is also already dead
    code — the lookup cannot match (TODO item 17).

  * Path normalisation must stay limited to traversal safety. It currently also
    converts backslashes to forward slashes, which IS a rewrite. Deliberate and
    probably right, but confirm.

  * The upload form and developer panel need rework: no typed asset name,
    version selection, clash prompts.


================================================================================
9. OPEN ITEMS
================================================================================

  * Precise meaning of "merge" and "add" in the per-path clash options.
  * The near-match threshold — keep it conservative; one named constant.
  * Where the known-extension list lives. It should be ONE registry serving
    both the name validator and the content-based type check.
  * Whether version-number gaps matter (v01, v02, v05) — take at face value,
    or flag?
  * Backslash-to-forward-slash normalisation: confirm it is wanted.
